<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<Description>Dynamic filtering, sorting and paging for IQueryable, driven by data rather than code. A condition built in code, parsed from a compact query language, or deserialized from a client becomes an expression tree and is applied to a query. You declare which properties are filterable, and callers can filter on those and nothing else. Works with EF Core, where conditions become SQL with values passed as parameters, and in memory over the same data. No dependencies.</Description>
		<PackageTags>iqueryable;linq;efcore;entity-framework-core;filtering;sorting;paging;expression-trees;dynamic-query;query-language</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>
Weequery 3.2.0.

BREAKING. Targets .NET 10 and .NET 11. .NET 8 is no longer supported; stay on 3.1.x for it.

BEHAVIOUR. BindResolve now binds every collection of values it finds, a List&lt;string&gt; or an int[], for the
quantifiers as well as a property, so its output gains keys. IgnorePaths "Tags[]" takes the quantifier away and
keeps the property. A byte[] and a dictionary are not collections of values.

Added: Any, All and None over a collection of values, naming the element Value (QuantifiedCondition.ValueKey):
Tags Any (Value = 'red'). BindCollection binds it on its own for such a collection, and
CollectionBindingSet.BindValue() does it by hand; the element type no longer has to be a class. A collection of
values still projects, and EF Core translates the quantifiers as primitive collections.

Clearer: a quantifier naming "Assignments[]" rather than "Assignments" is refused as KeyInvalid, with the
condition to write instead, where it used to fail as an empty index.

The companion packages are released alongside at the same version.
		</PackageReleaseNotes>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
